**Y1. The implemented wide-box search is a class-group lattice sieve.** There is a stronger conclusion for uniform sampling than C gave in round one. Write \(d=|D|\), \(B=L_d[a]\), and take a box of side \(K=L_d[1-a]\). A balanced reduced form has \(Q(x,y)\ll\sqrt d\,K^2=X\). Each integral ideal accounts for at most a unit orbit of representations in its class. For a uniform class and a uniform box point, the probability of a balanced form **and** a smooth hit is therefore at most
\[
\frac{O(X^s)}{h(D)K^2}\prod_{\ell\le B}(1-\ell^{-s})^{-2}.
\]
With C’s choice of \(s<1\), \(K^{2s}/K^2\le1\). The GRH bound remains \(L_d[1-a]^{-1}\), up to constants in the exponent. Widening the box can supply witnesses while leaving their density just as poor. Charging visits and base size gives exponent at least \(\max(a,1-a)\ge1/2\) for this sampling approach. Theorem SS explicitly excludes output-sensitive listing, so a decoder that jumps to a witness remains open. The 476 versus 70 count omits multiplier selection and relation rank. The earlier decoder spent 554 lattice reductions per hit against 474 direct norm tests. [C’s counting argument and toy](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/codex/equivtarget_1_codex_harvest.md:15) · [SS model](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/notes/suffmap_results.md:118) · [M2 comparison](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/notes/subthird_results.md:75)

**Y2. The \(j\)-only oracle is Rabin-type on C’s promise.** Away from collisions, each returned neighbour matches the private edge with probability \(1/3\) independently at each prime. Exactly one match gives a factor with probability \(4/9\), even for a deterministic oracle. Factoring gives the reverse algorithm in randomized polynomial time. G’s quadratic calculation requires an additional input, a root \(t\) of \((T+16)^3-jT\). After division, the discriminant is \((t+16)^2(t+64)/t\), so choosing a companion amounts to extracting \(\sqrt{t(t+64)}\) on the nonexceptional locus. Labelled 2-torsion gives an easy edge, and coprime-degree neighbours with a unique common completion give a linear polynomial gcd. Neither report constructs such auxiliary data from the disclosed \(j\) while retaining the independent private edge. [C’s promise](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/codex/equivtarget_1_codex_harvest.md:23) · [G’s quadratic](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/codex/equivtarget_1_gemini2.out:46)

**Y3. G’s lattice is the classical simultaneous-approximation lattice for approximate common divisors.** Set the exact sample \(x_0=N\) in equation (3.1) of Galbraith–Gebregiyorgis–Murphy. Small residues at both primes plant two short vectors. G’s displayed BKZ inequality points the wrong way: its inflated determinant scale cannot be below a planted vector already shorter than the determinant scale. The proposed exponent \(1-2\gamma\) is at best a gap heuristic, with logarithmic slack and a further requirement that reduction recover a factor-bearing multiplier. [G’s ledger](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/codex/equivtarget_1_gemini.out:18) · [Classical lattice, §3](https://doi.org/10.1112/S1461157016000218)

There **is** a natural \(N\)-only bias on a promised family. For \(p,q\equiv1\bmod d\), take \(x_i=(N-1)/d+i\). Its local residues are \((p-1)/d+i\) and \((q-1)/d+i\), small and unequal. Yet \(dx_i\equiv di-1\bmod N\). In the preregistered 16-sample test, the public coprime multiplier \(d=2^{14}\) gave vector length \(4.30\times10^9\), versus over \(2.32\times10^{15}\) for either planted factor. All samples are public shifts of one value. For uniform \(x\), the joint event costs about \(2\delta\) bits of surprisal. That count alone establishes neither useful information about \(p\) nor factoring equivalence. An informative sampler on general balanced \(N\) remains missing. [Preregistration](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/codex/scratch/equiv_c/crossreview-prereg.txt:2) · [Exact results](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/codex/scratch/equiv_c/crossreview-results.txt:1)

**Y4. G’s claimed Kani “iff” has an algebraic counterexample.** In Eisenstein coordinates take \(N=403=13\cdot31\), \(e=19\), \(\mu=(12,11)\), and \(\nu=(21,2)\). The genuine \(\mu\nu=(230,233)\) and the alternative \(\eta=(197,-58)\) agree modulo 3 and both have norm \(133N\). Thus both matrices have \(F^\dagger F=2^{19}I\), give the polarized product quotient, and agree on all of \(E_0[3]\). Exhausting all 24 norm-\(403\) representations confirms that \(\eta\bmod 2^{19}\) belongs to neither the \(\mu\) family nor its conjugate. This checks the matrix identity directly. [Claim](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/codex/equivtarget_1_gemini2.out:10) · [Exact example](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/codex/scratch/equiv_c/crossreview-results.txt:3)

The intended candidates already admit a polynomial-time integer test: center-lift their coordinates, check exact norm \(mN\), then divide by \(\mu\). The coordinates have size \(O(\sqrt{mN})\), well within the lift. Two inequivalent norm-\(N\) representations split \(N\) by a gcd on the split-prime promise. Free torsion data include the determinant, the known CM action, and even an associate normalized to act identically on \(E_0[3]\), since the six units exhaust \((\mathbb Z[\omega]/3)^\times\). These constraints leave the unknown large \(2\)-power torsion image undetermined. Scanning its torus costs \(\Theta(2^e)\). For general \(N\), Jacobi \(+1\) also admits two inert primes. [Reduction](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/codex/equivtarget_1_gemini2.out:7) · [CM hypothesis](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/notes/tracedata_results.md:220)

**Y5.** Rank-two units preserve absolute norms and every finite-prime valuation. New parity rank requires inequivalent elements. G found zero qualifying vectors among 214 full-support candidates. Joux’s \(1/4\) algorithm uses very small characteristic. C’s anomalous component is a genuine special-family factorer, but \(D\le L[a]\) covers at most a \(p^{-1/2+o(1)}\) fraction of primes near \(p\). G’s higher-genus \(1/p\) claim remains unproved: a Weil interval spanning several residue periods supplies no equidistribution theorem. [Tower test](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/codex/equivtarget_1_gemini2.out:28) · [Joux](https://eprint.iacr.org/2013/095) · [Anomaly bounds](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/notes/nonsmooth_results.md:239)

**Y6.** Every nonzero ring map to a field is unital here because \(f(1)\) is a nonzero idempotent. Hence \(\ell\mid N\). MOV and Weil restriction preserve characteristic. Group-only maps escape: the Jacobi symbol already maps units into \(\mathbb F_3^\times\) without factoring. A useful transfer still needs a computable image and factor readout. Embedding a cyclic challenge of order \(r\) in \(\mathbb F_{\ell^k}^{\times}\) requires \(r\mid\ell^k-1\) at affordable \(k\). Neither report supplies that construction. [Transfer claim](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/codex/equivtarget_1_codex_harvest.md:55)

**Y7. Joint verdict.** Equivalence remains a productive screening question, but none of these six candidates currently yields general sub-\(L[1/3]\) factoring. My single next investigation would be an output-sensitive quadratic CRT decoder for M2 on blinded, near-uniform target classes, charging multiplier selection, partial assignments visited, and work per independent relation. I would cap the first comparison with direct sieving at one CPU-hour on 64–96-bit discriminants. My subjective probability that this route reaches \(L[a]\), \(a<1/3\), is **0.2%**.